      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Personalised User Narrative Experience",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Personalised User Narrative Experience",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "6gPk4EB3Amypv1DpZjDwe",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -4272.2322277972435,
      "y": 10281.796506381615,
      "width": 14.399993896484375,
      "height": 45,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bBi",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 366915886,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 1657519154,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1792216762417,
      "created": 1792216761371,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "CjujHfzCCbyCs1GR1yb5d",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -8792.620895573204,
      "y": 7862.03942181918,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 575,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bBj",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 330028978,
      "version": 1431,
      "versionNonce": 1812472077,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1792283591563,
      "created": 1792216762645,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "To effectively scale to large amounts of users, the in game narrative generation\ncannot rely on separate LLM calls for every single user, as this becomes far too\nexpensive. However, it is important that users receive a personalised narrative\nexperience. To reduce the amount of concurrent narratives that need to be\ngenerated by our narrative generation engine, we employ the use of user\ncategorisations. Instead of building narratives for every user, we build a matrix\nof user types based on their categorisations, and feed each user type the same\nnarrative that has been generated for that type.\n\nPersonalisation is only applied at the final step of narrative generation. The\nfacts of what happened in a match don't change depending on who you support,\nso the minute summaries and context compressions are built once per match and\nshared across all user types. Only the narrative author step is run per user type.\nThis keeps costs down, guarantees every user type is working from the same set\nof facts, and means a user type that tunes in late in the match can have a\nnarrative generated immediately from the shared context.\n\nBelow is an example of a simple categorisation that we are using for the\napplication. A user is either a fan of the home team, away team or is neutral (this\ncan be inferred from knowing the team the user supports as selected in the\nfrontend and the teams that are currently playing). Also, a user may want\ndifferent complexities of analysis in their narrative, ranging from Simple to\nAnalyst.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "To effectively scale to large amounts of users, the in game narrative generation cannot rely on separate LLM calls for every single user, as this becomes far too expensive. However, it is important that users receive a personalised narrative experience. To reduce the amount of concurrent narratives that need to be generated by our narrative generation engine, we employ the use of user categorisations. Instead of building narratives for every user, we build a matrix of user types based on their categorisations, and feed each user type the same narrative that has been generated for that type.\n\nPersonalisation is only applied at the final step of narrative generation. The facts of what happened in a match don't change depending on who you support, so the minute summaries and context compressions are built once per match and shared across all user types. Only the narrative author step is run per user type. This keeps costs down, guarantees every user type is working from the same set of facts, and means a user type that tunes in late in the match can have a narrative generated immediately from the shared context.\n\nBelow is an example of a simple categorisation that we are using for our demo project. A user is either a fan of the home team, away team or is neutral (this can be inferred from knowing the team the user supports as selected in the frontend and the teams that are currently playing). Also, a user may want different complexities of analysis in their narrative, ranging from Simple to Analyst.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "OafOeQXsQU4Ah5mSb8GGD",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -4286.331661204901,
      "y": 11255.689564260138,
      "width": 8,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bBk",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 474178606,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 1063549234,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1792217109389,
      "created": 1792217108550,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "yxTVBqatqXCovJACsSfwH",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -8790.163093024956,
      "y": 9103.925048487454,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 200,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bBl",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1694061746,
      "version": 581,
      "versionNonce": 1708816237,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1792283591563,
      "created": 1792217109695,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "There are 12 possible combinations of user types as shown in the matrix above,\nso regardless of how many users our application serves there is only ever a\nmaximum of 12 narrative excerpts generated per narrative step. These will be\nlazily generated to further reduce redundant computation, meaning only user\ntypes that have at least one user tuning in will have their narrative generated.\nBecause the match context is shared, the minute summarisation and context\ncompression agents are each only ever called once per scheduled step, no matter\nhow many user types are active.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "There are 12 possible combinations of user types as shown in the matrix above, so regardless of how many users our application serves there is only ever a maximum of 12 narrative excerpts generated per narrative step. These will be lazily generated to further reduce redundant computation, meaning only user types that have at least one user tuning in will have their narrative generated. Because the match context is shared, the minute summarisation and context compression agents are each only ever called once per scheduled step, no matter how many user types are active.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "2LjF83bYaUQegubV91zeJ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -8792.732972307407,
      "y": 9335.525348697198,
      "width": 487.90777587890625,
      "height": 90,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bBm",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 841430318,
      "version": 379,
      "versionNonce": 1943208397,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1792283591563,
      "created": 1792217300241,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Narrative Generation Engine\n",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Narrative Generation Engine\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "9T301zNhFM92dpyiqd3bX",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -8778.622241823032,
      "y": 9445.401955895219,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 1075,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bBn",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1430146414,
      "version": 1851,
      "versionNonce": 453722157,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1792283591563,
      "created": 1792217331313,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The narrative generation engine utilises a multi agent orchestration that includes\na minute summarisation agent, a context compression agent, and a narrative\nauthor agent. The minute summarisation and context compression agents produce\na single shared, factual match context. The narrative author agent is the only\nagent that is personalised per user type.\n\nIt works as follows:\nEvery minute, the minute summarisation agent will look at the event log over the\nprevious minute, and the current match state statistics and summarise any key\ninformation into a concise, factual record of what occurred during that time.\n\nOn a set schedule, which is detailed in the context construction diagrams below,\nthe context compression agent will compress the context of old minute\nsummarisations together, to decrease the tokens used for events further and\nfurther into the past and effectively reduce the weighting of these events to\ninform current narrative storylines.\n\nKey events (goals, red cards, penalties and VAR overturns) are not left to the\nLLM agents to keep track of. They are extracted directly from the live event\nstream as they arrive and stored in a key event ledger with their exact\ntimestamps. This ledger is passed verbatim to the narrative author agent so\nthat key events can never be dropped or mistimed through repeated\nsummarisation and compression.\n\nAfter key events, or 10 minutes after the previous narrative step, the narrative\nauthor agent will be called once for each active user type. Its context window\nconcatenates the earlier larger time frame compressions, the recent minute\nsummarisations, any raw events that have arrived since the last minute summary\n(so the event that triggered the call is always included, even mid minute), the key\nevent ledger, and the most recent narrative excerpts for that user type, to\nproduce the next step. This is what will go back to the user.\n\nSome additional rules:\n- Key events arriving close together (e.g. a goal followed by a VAR overturn) are\ndebounced over a short window so they produce one coherent narrative step\nrather than two contradicting ones.\n- Match time is tracked as (period, minute, added minute), so stoppage time such\nas 45+1' is handled consistently.\n- No minute summaries are produced during half time. The break is used to run\nany outstanding compressions and can optionally trigger a half time narrative\nstep.\n- All agents return structured JSON output so the python service can reliably\nparse and store their results.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The narrative generation engine utilises a multi agent orchestration that includes a minute summarisation agent, a context compression agent, and a narrative author agent. The minute summarisation and context compression agents produce a single shared, factual match context. The narrative author agent is the only agent that is personalised per user type.\n\nIt works as follows:\nEvery minute, the minute summarisation agent will look at the event log over the previous minute, and the current match state statistics and summarise any key information into a concise, factual record of what occurred during that time.\n\nOn a set schedule, which is detailed in the context construction diagrams below, the context compression agent will compress the context of old minute summarisations together, to decrease the tokens used for events further and further into the past and effectively reduce the weighting of these events to inform current narrative storylines.\n\nKey events (goals, red cards, penalties and VAR overturns) are not left to the LLM agents to keep track of. They are extracted directly from the live event stream as they arrive and stored in a key event ledger with their exact timestamps. This ledger is passed verbatim to the narrative author agent so that key events can never be dropped or mistimed through repeated summarisation and compression.\n\nAfter key events, or 10 minutes after the previous narrative step, the narrative author agent will be called once for each active user type. Its context window concatenates the earlier larger time frame compressions, the recent minute summarisations, any raw events that have arrived since the last minute summary (so the event that triggered the call is always included, even mid minute), the key event ledger, and the most recent narrative excerpts for that user type, to produce the next step. This is what will go back to the user.\n\nSome additional rules:\n- Key events arriving close together (e.g. a goal followed by a VAR overturn) are debounced over a short window so they produce one coherent narrative step rather than two contradicting ones.\n- Match time is tracked as (period, minute, added minute), so stoppage time such as 45+1' is handled consistently.\n- No minute summaries are produced during half time. The break is used to run any outstanding compressions and can optionally trigger a half time narrative step.\n- All agents return structured JSON output so the python service can reliably parse and store their results.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
