      "text": "- minute summarisation agent called every minute\n- context compression agent called on 36th to 40th minute summaries\n- context compression agent called on 1st to 30th minute summaries",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- minute summarisation agent called every minute\n- context compression agent called on 36th to 40th minute summaries\n- context compression agent called on 1st to 30th minute summaries",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "K2vrBEK2uLUAMEHo9BFDc",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -8749.030724403307,
      "y": 12317.051157631744,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 1025,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bEd",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 577724082,
      "version": 2806,
      "versionNonce": 528461165,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1792283591563,
      "created": 1792218823174,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The diagram above shows how the shared match context fed into the narrative\nauthor agent is constructed throughout the match. Every minute, the minute\nsummarisation agent looks at the events that have occurred in the previous\nminute and the current game state statistics to produce a short summary of the\nkey moments of the previous minute. When the narrative author agent is required\nto add to the match narrative, it needs to have the context of the match to\nthat point, so the naive way of doing this would be to feed all the minute\nsummaries in the match so far to the agent for it to build a narrative out of.\nHowever this has two problems:\n1. Economics - token count becomes very large very quickly. In the 80th minute\nof the match there will be 80 summaries to read as well as all the previous\nnarrative entries which will add to many tokens.\n2. Narrative focus - filling out the context window with summaries from every\nminute diverts the focus of the narrative away from what is currently happening\ntowards giving equal weighting to events spanning the entirety of the game.\nWhilst previous events in a match do play in to the storylines that are relevant\nlater in a game, compressing the amount of tokens spent towards the early\nstages of matches and spending proportionally more of the context window on\nthe current state will better guide the LLM towards what is relevant now.\n\nTo answer these problems, we designed the context construction algorithm\nabove. The basic idea behind it is that after a period of time, earlier one minute\nsummaries are less relevant and so the context compression agent is given a set\nof summaries to compress into one larger time window summary. As the game\ncontinues, this keeps on happening in the background so that the context\nwindow includes between 6 to 10 of the most recent one minute summaries, and\nthen ever increasing larger time window summaries from earlier on.\n\nThe compression schedule follows three simple rules:\n1. When 11 minute summaries are held, the oldest 5 are compressed into a 5 minute\nblock.\n2. When 4 five minute blocks are held, the oldest 2 are merged together with the\nexisting earlier match block (if there is one) into a single earlier match block.\n3. Each summary or compression block records the time window it covers, and\nwhen it is compressed it is marked as superseded by the new block.\n\nTo build the context window at any point, the python service simply takes every\nblock that has not been superseded, ordered by time. This always covers the\nmatch from kickoff to now with no gaps or overlaps, and the context window\nstays bounded at one earlier match block, up to three 5 minute blocks and 6 to\n10 minute summaries, no matter how long the match runs.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The diagram above shows how the shared match context fed into the narrative author agent is constructed throughout the match. Every minute, the minute summarisation agent looks at the events that have occurred in the previous minute and the current game state statistics to produce a short summary of the key moments of the previous minute. When the narrative author agent is required to add to the match narrative, it needs to have the context of the match to that point, so the naive way of doing this would be to feed all the minute summaries in the match so far to the agent for it to build a narrative out of. However this has two problems:\n1. Economics - token count becomes very large very quickly. In the 80th minute of the match there will be 80 summaries to read as well as all the previous narrative entries which will add to many tokens.\n2. Narrative focus - filling out the context window with summaries from every minute diverts the focus of the narrative away from what is currently happening towards giving equal weighting to events spanning the entirety of the game. Whilst previous events in a match do play in to the storylines that are relevant later in a game, compressing the amount of tokens spent towards the early stages of matches and spending proportionally more of the context window on the current state will better guide the LLM towards what is relevant now.\n\nTo answer these problems, we designed the context construction algorithm above. The basic idea behind it is that after a period of time, earlier one minute summaries are less relevant and so the context compression agent is given a set of summaries to compress into one larger time window summary. As the game continues, this keeps on happening in the background so that the context window includes between 6 to 10 of the most recent one minute summaries, and then ever increasing larger time window summaries from earlier on.\n\nThe compression schedule follows three simple rules:\n1. When 11 minute summaries are held, the oldest 5 are compressed into a 5 minute block.\n2. When 4 five minute blocks are held, the oldest 2 are merged together with the existing earlier match block (if there is one) into a single earlier match block.\n3. Each summary or compression block records the time window it covers, and when it is compressed it is marked as superseded by the new block.\n\nTo build the context window at any point, the python service simply takes every block that has not been superseded, ordered by time. This always covers the match from kickoff to now with no gaps or overlaps, and the context window stays bounded at one earlier match block, up to three 5 minute blocks and 6 to 10 minute summaries, no matter how long the match runs.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
